# Line endings are content here, not formatting.
#
# Git normalises line endings on checkout when `core.autocrlf` is set, which is
# the default on Windows installs. For ordinary source that is invisible and
# harmless. For a file whose bytes are pinned by a digest it is silent
# corruption: the working tree differs from what was hashed, every recorded
# digest is wrong, and nothing reports it — the file still parses, still reads
# correctly, and still looks exactly like itself.
#
# `-text` disables the conversion in both directions regardless of the user's
# `core.autocrlf`.
#
# This is the same class the formatter is kept out of in `biome.jsonc`, and the
# rule stated there applies unchanged: the rule is the class, not the file. A
# repository that stores captured artefacts beside its own source has two kinds
# of file with opposite rules. Anything added to one ignore list belongs in the
# other.

# The relay corpus. Each record cites its parent by `parent-sha256`, so a
# rewritten byte anywhere breaks the chain from that record forward — and the
# break would be discovered by a verifier, long after the checkout that caused it.
relay/** -text

# Bytes pinned by `corpus/manifest.json`.
corpus/** -text

# Retrieval evidence, pinned in `docs/experiments/debian-rb-retrieval.md`.
docs/experiments/debian-rb-bytes/** -text

# Material handed to blind readers. Changing what a reader was shown, after the
# fact, makes their report unattributable to anything.
docs/experiments/clause-reader/corpus/** -text
docs/experiments/suite-audit/suite/** -text

# RFC 8785's reference vectors. Their whole value is being byte-for-byte what
# someone else produced, and `tests/relay-lite-rfc8785-vectors.test.ts` compares
# byte-for-byte — so a rewrite would not fail the test, it would rewrite the
# question and the answer together.
tests/fixtures/rfc8785/** -text

# Frozen source: the issue #5 thread the lineage work greps against. Byte-exact,
# so counts in relay-0758 and after are reproducible.
docs/sources/** -text
